# Feature name: duration_bin
# Feature description: Binned duration into categories (e.g., [short-term, medium-term, long-term]) based on quantiles (Q1, Median, Q3) of its values.
# Usefulness: Helps capture the duration's relative significance without its raw numerical scale, which can aid categorical classifiers or models prone to overfitting on high cardinal values like random forests.

import pandas as pd
df['duration_bin'] = pd.cut(
    df['duration'], 
    bins=[4, 12, 24, 60], 
    labels=['short-term', 'medium-term', 'long-term'], 
    include_lowest=True
)
end

python
# Feature name: credit_amount_to_income_ratio
# Feature description: New feature calculated as credit_amount divided by installment_commitment, representing the ratio of credit requested to monthly disposable income.
# Usefulness: Provides a financial insight into the individual's ability to afford the credit. Higher ratios might indicate riskier profiles, allowing better prediction performance for creditworthiness.

df['credit_amount_to_income_ratio'] = df['credit_amount'] / (df['installment_commitment'] * 100)
end

python
# Feature name: age_bin
# Feature description: Binned age into categories (e.g., [youth, middle-aged, elderly]) based on quantiles (Q1, Median, Q3).
# Usefulness: Smooths the relationship between age and credit-approval prediction by reducing variance in models sensitive to raw age data while capturing age-related behavioral trends on credit decisions.

df['age_bin'] = pd.cut(
    df['age'], 
    bins=[19, 27, 42, 75], 
    labels=['youth', 'middle-aged', 'elderly'], 
    include_lowest=True
)
end

python
# Explanation why the column own_telephone is dropped
# This column represents whether the applicant has a telephone (yes or none). It is redundant, as modern credit evaluations rarely factor telephone ownership into creditworthiness.
df.drop(columns=['own_telephone'], inplace=True)
end

python
# Explanation why the column foreign_worker is dropped
# foreign_worker might introduce biases in the model, besides being binary (i.e., yes/no) with little variability. It likely adds minimal discriminative value to the task, as employment details already encapsulate the worker's stability.
df.drop(columns=['foreign_worker'], inplace=True)
end

